  "comments": [
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9a91a2c5_6386fd0e",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1512,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869780
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-08T19:22:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Instead of optimizing the code path for requeuing I/O, it is better to prevent that requeuing happens. Same comment for blk_mq_delay_kick_requeue_list().",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1508,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1512,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e52d1c5a_2c3dd7a6",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1512,
      "author": {
        "id": 1879222
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-09T03:59:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The frequent gating and ungating of the clock by the UFS controller makes it impossible to prevent requeueing. Our goal is to minimize scheduler latency when requeueing occurs.",
      "parentUuid": "9a91a2c5_6386fd0e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1508,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1512,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2cd5d737_8213ca92",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1512,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869780
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-09T16:39:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "With which kernel version has this been observed? The following patch has been merged in kernel v6.5 and prevents that the clock gating mechanism triggers requeuing: \n```\ncommit 078f4f4b34d6c2dadabb363d3fc6c84b32927dea\nAuthor: Bart Van Assche \u003cbvanassche@acm.org\u003e\nDate:   Mon May 29 13:26:40 2023 -0700\n\n    scsi: ufs: Ungate the clock synchronously\n    \n    Ungating the clock asynchronously causes ufshcd_queuecommand() to return\n    SCSI_MLQUEUE_HOST_BUSY and hence causes commands to be requeued.  This is\n    suboptimal. Allow ufshcd_queuecommand() to sleep such that clock ungating\n    does not trigger command requeuing. Remove the ufshcd_scsi_block_requests()\n    and ufshcd_scsi_unblock_requests() calls because these are no longer\n    needed. The flush_work(\u0026hba-\u003eclk_gating.ungate_work) call is sufficient to\n    make the SCSI core wait for clock ungating to complete.\n    \n    Acked-by: Adrian Hunter \u003cadrian.hunter@intel.com\u003e\n    Signed-off-by: Bart Van Assche \u003cbvanassche@acm.org\u003e\n    Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/20230529202640.11883-6-bvanassche@acm.org\n    Reviewed-by: Bean Huo \u003cbeanhuo@micron.com\u003e\n    Reviewed-by: Bao D. Nguyen \u003cquic_nguyenb@quicinc.com\u003e\n    Signed-off-by: Martin K. Petersen \u003cmartin.petersen@oracle.com\u003e\n```",
      "parentUuid": "e52d1c5a_2c3dd7a6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1508,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1512,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "956b1875_c0463a6d",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1512,
      "author": {
        "id": 1879222
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-10T08:31:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The observation of clock gating and ungating causing requeuing occurred in kernel versions prior to v6.1. However, requeueing persists when user space triggers fsync. Certain applications perform fsync on their main thread during startup, and the prolonged scheduler latency of kblockd workqueue undoubtedly impacts the speed of application startup.",
      "parentUuid": "2cd5d737_8213ca92",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1508,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1512,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ca15c033_752d2245",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1512,
      "author": {
        "id": 1724998
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-15T20:02:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do you still observe this issue in the recent kernels 6.6+ which should have the Bart\u0027s patch ?",
      "parentUuid": "956b1875_c0463a6d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1508,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1512,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ad1ba803_5b16888d",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1512,
      "author": {
        "id": 1952910
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-16T03:55:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Bart\u0027s patch has significantly improved such issues. However, a similar issue still occurs when dealing with fsync. This can be seen in the code: blk_kick_flush()-\u003eblk_mq_kick_requeue_list(q).",
      "parentUuid": "ca15c033_752d2245",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1508,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1512,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "38c7416c_a7b2baa8",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1512,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-18T19:30:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We\u0027ll move ahead with this for 6.6. Please work on a general solution that can be upstreamed for 6.12.",
      "parentUuid": "ad1ba803_5b16888d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1508,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1512,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "34aab2f8_3a0894f0",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 2269,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869780
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-08T19:22:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Since we know that a new queue run will only happen after a delay, it is not clear to me why it would even matter on which CPU the delayed run work is scheduled?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2266,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2269,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f9efdb18_eafa7c08",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 2269,
      "author": {
        "id": 1879222
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-09T03:59:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We have extensively customized the CFS (Completely Fair Scheduler) scheduler and introduced a workqueue with higher priority than the default kblockd workqueue. In our testing environment, we have observed instances where the kblockd workqueue experiences scheduler latencies ranging from dozens to hundreds of milliseconds. Our primary goal is to utilize this customized workqueue to reduce scheduler latency.",
      "parentUuid": "34aab2f8_3a0894f0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2266,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2269,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "652a099d_a4e30380",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 2269,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869780
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-09T16:39:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why does it matter how quickly blk_mq_delay_run_hw_queue() is run? Is this function perhaps called frequently? If so, why is it called frequently? Is this because ufshcd_queuecommand() returns a non-zero value, is this because scsi_queue_rq() returns BLK_STS_RESOURCE or BLK_STS_DEV_RESOURCE or does this perhaps have another reason?\n\nIf an I/O scheduler is active, does reducing /sys/class/block/.../queue/nr_requests to the queue depth supported by the UFS driver help (31 in case of UFSHCI 3.1)?",
      "parentUuid": "f9efdb18_eafa7c08",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2266,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2269,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7186f094_01ea3085",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 2269,
      "author": {
        "id": 1879222
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-10T08:31:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "blk_mq_delay_run_hw_queue() is triggered by several scenarios: \n1. When the plug is NULL\n2. Upon exhaustion of tags for the UFS controller, invoking blk_mq_dispatch_wake()-\u003eblk_mq_run_hw_queue(hctx, true)\n\nThe critical factor is not the frequency of this function being called, but rather the potential for the kworker to be in a runnable state for dozens or even hundreds of milliseconds when blk_mq_delay_run_hw_queue() is triggered. Regardless of the low probability of this occurrence, when this happens, its impact on performance would be substantial. \n\nImagine that if the kworker thread accidentally remains in the runnable state for hundreds of milliseconds, it would lead to the phone becoming slow to respond with a high probability.",
      "parentUuid": "652a099d_a4e30380",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2266,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2269,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "194041c5_33303a9d",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 2269,
      "author": {
        "id": 1724998
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-15T20:02:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "First - I see Bart had some fair questions but wasn\u0027t fully answered. I would appreciate to keep the comments open unless it is agreed upon. Thanks.\n\n\u003e The critical factor is not the frequency of this function being called, but rather the potential for the kworker to be in a runnable state for dozens or even hundreds of milliseconds \n\nThe issue related to kworker scheduling is a much wider beyond this subsystem.\n\n1: Given that you mention that you have a completely re-designed CFS scheduler, you would have to start injecting such vendor hooks all over the storage stack. What guarantees that this specific vendor hook is just going to address the issue and not touch other layers of the storage stack.",
      "parentUuid": "7186f094_01ea3085",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2266,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2269,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f4941f2e_7f1739d9",
        "filename": "block/blk-mq.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 2269,
      "author": {
        "id": 1952910
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-16T03:55:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e First - I see Bart had some fair questions but wasn\u0027t fully answered. I would appreciate to keep the comments open unless it is agreed upon. Thanks.\n\u003e \n\u003e Is this because ufshcd_queuecommand() returns a non-zero value, is this because scsi_queue_rq() returns BLK_STS_RESOURCE or BLK_STS_DEV_RESOURCE or does this perhaps have another reason?\n\nNo, I don\u0027t think so.\nblk_mq_delay_run_hw_queue() is triggered by several scenarios:\n1. When the plug is NULL\n2. When io_schedule is called\n3. When invoking blk_mq_dispatch_wake()-\u003eblk_mq_run_hw_queue(hctx, true)\n\n\u003e If an I/O scheduler is active, does reducing /sys/class/block/.../queue/nr_requests to the queue depth supported by the UFS driver help (31 in case of UFSHCI 3.1)?\n\nNo, I think it is unrelated to the queue depth.\n\n\u003e 1: Given that you mention that you have a completely re-designed CFS scheduler, you would have to start injecting such vendor hooks all over the storage stack. What guarantees that this specific vendor hook is just going to address the issue and not touch other layers of the storage stack.\n\nWe have added certain flags to indicate when the issue may occur. These vendor hooks are only functional when these flags are set. Otherwise, the handling remains the same as the native kernel.",
      "parentUuid": "194041c5_33303a9d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2266,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2269,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "c2160e6ab424c1ac31c6a61c1d55bcb8df57b661",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
